  "strategy": "1. **Approach and rationale**\nThe primary task is to minimize the maximum overlap integral defined as C\u2085 for the Erd\u0151s problem. The previous GA approach may struggle with local optima and computational inefficiency. Our strategy employs iterative convex optimization with a line search to navigate the non-convexity more effectively.\n\nCore idea:\n- Represent h as a step function over [0,2] with constraints \u222bh=1 and 0\u2264h\u22641.\n- Use CVXPY to solve a sequence of convex approximations, iteratively tightening constraints.\n- Exploit problem structure by linearizing non-convex cross-correlation terms.\n- No need for complex mutation/crossover; use optimization techniques.\n\n2. **Mathematical specification**\nDefine variables:\n- h \u2208 \u211d\u207f (n = n_points) where h_i \u2208 [0,1]\n- C \u2208 \u211d (the objective to minimize)\n\nObjective:\nMinimize C subject to:\n1. \u2211h_i = n/2\n2. for all k \u2208 [1, n-1], \u2211h_i(1 - h_{i+k}) \u2264 C (discretized form)\n\nNon-convexity arises from h_i h_{i+k} terms. We use a majorization-minimization approach, solving a convex subproblem at each step:\n- Linearize h_{i+k} as h\u00b2_{i+k} \u2248 h_{i+k}^old * h_i\n\n3. **Implementation procedure**\na. Parent validation:\n   - If 'initial_h_values' exists, validate and normalize to [0,1] and \u2211h = n/2.\n   - Else, initialize h to 0.5 for all points, ensuring feasibility.\n\nb. Iterative convex optimization loop:\n   i. For current h^old, precompute all k-shift constraints.\n   ii. Formulate convex approximation:\n      - Variables: h \u2208 \u211d\u207f, C \u2208 \u211d\n      - Constraints: \u2211h = n/2 and linearized shifts\n      - Objective: minimize C\n   iii. Solve with CVXPY (SCS or ECOS)\n   iv. Apply line search to adaptively adjust step size for stability\n   v. Check constraints, project back if necessary (clip and rescale)\n   vi. Update best solution if feasible improvement found\n\nc. Termination:\n   - Max iterations (100)\n   - Convergence tolerance (change < 1e-5)\n   - Time budget exhausted\n\n4. **Search choices and useful variations**\n- Initial step size: 0.5\n- Line search ratio: 0.5 (backtracking)\n- Solver tolerance: 1e-4\n- Maximum iterations: 100\n- Feasibility tolerance: 1e-5 (for sum and bounds)\n- Clip after update to preserve h_i \u2208 [0,1]\n\n5. **Compute budget and robustness**\nEstimate 600s runtime with CVXPY solving 100 convex subproblems each taking ~5s. Includes feasibility projection and line search steps. Robust against infeasible proposals via clipping and rescaling.\n\n6. **Validation and return contract**\nFinal check:\n- \u2211h = n/2 \u00b1 1e-5\n- 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n- Compute actual C\u2085 via correlate(h, 1-h) to validate against CVXPY result\n- Return with default precision as per evaluator",
